<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
Spartrak — PDP (Figma 526:21854 / 532:15187 / 534:9322).

Only TWO additions. Porto's own catalog_product_view.xml is otherwise left
completely alone: its layout="2columns-right", every <move> it performs, and
its custom blocks all still apply. The Figma PDP is a full-width two-column
page (media 816 | 48 gutter | info 480, inside the 1344 band), and that is
achieved in CSS by re-flowing .column.main rather than by re-cutting the page
layout here — changing layout="2columns-right" to "1column" would orphan
every block Porto moves into sidebar.additional and is a bigger, separate
decision.

  spartrak.pdp.head    goes FIRST inside product.info.media (a core Magento
                       container) for two reasons: it is the earliest point in
                       the PDP's own content, so the temporary inline <style>
                       it carries lands before the styled markup; and being a
                       child of `.product.media` is what lets the Figma media
                       overlay (discount badge 526:23655 + the three round
                       action buttons 526:23305) position itself over the
                       gallery without touching Porto's 528-line gallery.phtml
                       or the Fotorama widget inside it.

  spartrak.pdp.options goes LAST inside product.info.main — the Figma
                       "خيارات المنتج" card (526:23403), which sits below the
                       add-to-cart row.

Both target containers are declared by Magento core, not by Porto, so neither
depends on Porto's layout surviving unchanged.
-->
<page xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="urn:magento:framework:View/Layout/etc/page_configuration.xsd">
    <body>
        <referenceContainer name="product.info.media">
            <block class="Magento\Catalog\Block\Product\View"
                   name="spartrak.pdp.head"
                   before="-"
                   template="Magento_Catalog::product/view/spartrak-pdp.phtml"/>
        </referenceContainer>
        <referenceContainer name="product.info.main">
            <block class="Magento\Catalog\Block\Product\View"
                   name="spartrak.pdp.options"
                   after="-"
                   template="Magento_Catalog::product/view/spartrak-options.phtml"/>
        </referenceContainer>

        <move
            element="catalog.product.related"
            destination="content"
            after="product.info.details"/>

        <!--
          RELATED PRODUCTS — Figma 535:12047 (desktop) / 655:19767 (mobile).

          The <move> above is what makes the block render at all. This only
          changes how it LOOKS: the class and the template. The block is still
          Magento's own related-products block, so what it shows stays exactly
          what an admin picked under a product's "Related Products" tab, in
          their order — and the template it now points at is the SAME rail the
          homepage renders (same header partial, same card, same CSS, same
          carousel widget).

          `limit` caps how many cards reach the HTML; related products are
          unbounded in the admin, and forty linked products would otherwise be
          forty cards, images and add-to-cart forms in the PDP's response.
          Changing it is this one line, no PHP.
        -->
        <referenceBlock name="catalog.product.related"
                        class="Spartrak\Catalog\Block\Product\RelatedRail"
                        template="Spartrak_Catalog::product/related-rail.phtml">
            <arguments>
                <argument name="limit" xsi:type="number">12</argument>
            </arguments>
        </referenceBlock>
    </body>
</page>
